---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Configuration formats — dotcfg"
  description="Use TOML, JSON, or YAML with one dotcfg API."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">Configuration</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Configuration formats
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      dotcfg keeps the API the same while the file format changes. TOML is enabled by default; JSON
      and YAML are opt-in Cargo features.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      TOML — default
    </h2>
    <CodeBlock label="Cargo.toml" code={'[dependencies]\ndotcfg = "0.3.0"'} />
    <p>
      <code>DotCfg::new("mytool")</code> selects TOML when the default features are enabled. You can
      also call <code>.toml()</code> explicitly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      JSON
    </h2>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Cargo.toml"
      code={'[dependencies]\ndotcfg = { version = "0.3.0", features = ["json"] }'}
    />
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={'let cfg = DotCfg::new("mytool").json();\n// ~/.mytool/config.json'}
    />

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      YAML
    </h2>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Cargo.toml"
      code={'[dependencies]\ndotcfg = { version = "0.3.0", features = ["yaml"] }'}
    />
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={'let cfg = DotCfg::new("mytool").yaml();\n// ~/.mytool/config.yaml'}
    />

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Enable several formats
    </h2>
    <p>
      Cargo features are additive. Enabling JSON does not disable the default TOML feature. If your
      program needs all three formats, enable JSON and YAML alongside the default.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Cargo.toml"
      code={'dotcfg = { version = "0.3.0", features = ["json", "yaml"] }'}
    />

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      One value model across formats
    </h2>
    <p>
      dotcfg parses TOML, JSON, and YAML into one internal value tree before key access, typed
      reads, or layered merging. That keeps <code>get()</code>, <code>get_as()</code>,{' '}
      <code>set()</code>, <code>set_val()</code>, <code>load()</code>, and <code>save()</code>{' '}
      aligned across formats instead of maintaining separate behavior for each parser.
    </p>
    <div class="my-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-2.5 max-[760px]:grid-cols-1">
      <div class="rounded-[9px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <strong class="mb-[7px] block">TOML</strong>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Arrays are written in inline array form. TOML datetime values are preserved as opaque values.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-[9px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <strong class="mb-[7px] block">YAML</strong>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Output maps are deterministic, and non-string mapping keys are rejected rather than coerced.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Callout type="info" title="serde_json is part of the core value layer">
      <p>
        The shared value representation uses <code>serde_json::Value</code> internally, so{' '}
        <code>serde_json</code> is now a normal dependency even when your application only stores
        TOML or YAML files.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Use your own filename
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>.filename()</code> accepts the filename without an extension. dotcfg adds the extension
      that matches the selected format.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={
        'let cfg = DotCfg::new("mytool")\n    .json()\n    .filename("settings");\n\n// ~/.mytool/settings.json'
      }
    />

    <Callout type="warning" title="At least one format is required">
      <p>
        If you disable default features, enable <code>toml</code>, <code>json</code>, or{' '}
        <code>yaml</code> yourself. dotcfg intentionally fails compilation when no format feature is
        available.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
